          "type": "turn",
          "question": "For heart failure, is there a code that has to come before or after it, and why?",
          "answer": "Yes, in one case. When heart failure appears together with high blood pressure that has affected the heart, the classification uses a combination code first, and the heart failure code comes second. The coding guide for hypertension says that documented hypertensive heart disease moves the case to code I11, and that the type of heart failure is then added from I50 as a second code (source: \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\").\n\nThe heart failure guide (source: \"Coding heart failure: I50, its mechanism axis and its acuity axis\") describes I50 itself. It is built from two details the record must state: the type of heart failure (systolic, diastolic, or combined) and how sudden it is (acute, chronic, or acute on chronic). Its position in the chapter, at the end of the \"other forms of heart disease\" block, is a matter of where it sits in the list, not an ordering rule.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not explain why I11 comes before I50 beyond saying the classification prefers the combination code. They also do not cover ordering when heart failure appears with other conditions, such as lung or kidney disease. For that, a medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM guidelines would need to be checked. Questions about a specific record should go to whoever handles that record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
